1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,500
NebulaChaos: a correctness lab for my Raft cluster, NebulaOS.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,800
Part 2 of the series. Part 1 showed it survives crashes.

3
00:00:07,321 --> 00:00:10,621
NebulaOS heals from a leader crash in 3.2 seconds.

4
00:00:10,721 --> 00:00:13,921
But surviving a crash is not the same as staying correct.

5
00:00:14,021 --> 00:00:17,321
So I built a tester whose only job is to make the cluster lie.

6
00:00:17,835 --> 00:00:21,535
Five real NebulaOS processes, a proxy on each of the 20 network links.

7
00:00:21,635 --> 00:00:25,635
Clients read, write and compare-and-set while the nemesis breaks things.

8
00:00:25,735 --> 00:00:29,835
Every operation is recorded, then checked: could one correct machine have done this?

9
00:00:30,345 --> 00:00:36,345
Bug 1. First run against upstream NebulaOS.

10
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:39,353
nebulachaos run: 10 clients, and the leader is cut off twice.

11
00:00:39,453 --> 00:00:42,153
Real output, sped up. The checker reads all 9,000 operations.

12
00:00:42,253 --> 00:00:45,218
Not linearizable, on every key.

13
00:00:45,468 --> 00:00:48,468
A replay of that exact run, 0.16 s in. No fault injected yet.

14
00:00:48,568 --> 00:00:52,868
write 7.5 is acknowledged. Then a read on n2 returns the older 5.3.

15
00:00:52,968 --> 00:00:57,568
A follower answered from its own copy, one heartbeat behind the leader.

16
00:00:58,318 --> 00:01:04,818
The fix: the ReadIndex protocol from Ongaro's Raft thesis.

17
00:01:05,324 --> 00:01:08,324
Same schedule, same seed, fixed build.

18
00:01:08,424 --> 00:01:12,364
Linearizable. 10 of 10 runs failed before the fix, 0 of 10 after.

19
00:01:12,714 --> 00:01:19,214
Bug 2. Under the first post, Ram pointed at durability. So I went after the disk.

20
00:01:19,722 --> 00:01:23,022
n4 and n5 are cut off. Only n1, n2 and n3 acknowledge writes.

21
00:01:23,122 --> 00:01:26,022
n1 and n3 crash. One bit flips in n2's Raft log.

22
00:01:26,122 --> 00:01:30,022
n2 silently drops 72 log entries, then wins an election with n4 and n5.

23
00:01:30,122 --> 00:01:37,422
An acknowledged write is gone: a later read returns the older value.

24
00:01:39,406 --> 00:01:45,906
The fix: tell corruption apart from a torn write, and refuse to start.

25
00:01:46,412 --> 00:01:50,312
Same schedule after the fix: n2 refuses to start.

26
00:01:50,412 --> 00:01:56,612
No quorum for 7.6 s until n1 and n3 return. Unavailable, never wrong.

27
00:01:57,235 --> 00:02:03,235
Bug 3 needed leader churn: the leader is killed every two seconds.

28
00:02:03,743 --> 00:02:06,843
Leaders keep dying while 20 clients write through every node.

29
00:02:06,943 --> 00:02:11,043
A client is told "cas mismatch", yet a read returns the very value that CAS wrote.

30
00:02:11,143 --> 00:02:16,043
Fix: a request id assigned once, so a retried command is applied at most once.

31
00:02:20,373 --> 00:02:24,473
Is the checker any good? I planted four known Raft bugs behind flags.

32
00:02:24,573 --> 00:02:28,873
It caught three. The fourth needs millisecond timing: a job for deterministic simulation.

33
00:02:29,382 --> 00:02:33,682
Each bug, on the same fault schedules, before and after its fix.

34
00:02:33,782 --> 00:02:38,882
After the fixes: 60 randomized fault runs, 284,732 operations, all linearizable.

35
00:02:39,389 --> 00:02:43,289
The protocol was fine. The bugs lived in the plumbing around it.

36
00:02:43,389 --> 00:02:47,389
Thank you for watching. Code and every run: github.com/coolstack-coder/NebulaChaos
